SUMMARY:Listening through the Seasons: Introduction to Soundscapes
DESCRIPTION:Listening through the Seasons: Introduction to Soundscapes\nwith Meg Worthington \nFriday\, January 8 | 1 – 3:30 pm \nBRN – Elective \nRegister Here!\nHow does the Arboretum sound in winter? What can we learn by listening closely? \nJoin us as we launch a yearlong exploration of the NC Arboretum’s changing soundscape. In this introductory session\, participants will discover the field of soundscape ecology\, learn how scientists use sound to study ecosystems\, and practice techniques for deep listening and observation. Together\, we’ll visit our first listening station\, collect baseline observations\, and begin documenting the sounds of winter. \nListening Through the Seasons is a yearlong community science and soundscape ecology project at the NC Arboretum. Participants will help document the changing sounds of the Arboretum throughout the year while learning about ecology\, biodiversity\, and the art of deep listening. Through field observations\, sound recording\, and guided activities\, we’ll create a seasonal acoustic portrait of the Arboretum and contribute to an ongoing research project exploring how natural soundscapes change over time. \nThis program includes instruction both inside a classroom and outside following easy trails and paved walkways. Dress for the weather and prepare to be outside for at least half of the class. \n Meg Worthington (they/them) is an educator\, artist\, and lifelong learner whose work is rooted in curiosity\, creativity\, and connection to the natural world. They began teaching in Alaska while earning a Master’s degree in Outdoor and Environmental Education\, where they discovered a passion for creating meaningful experiences that inspire wonder and exploration. Working on a small educational farm in Palmer\, Alaska\, deepened Meg’s appreciation for the relationships between people\, place\, and community. Today\, they are enjoying the opportunity to learn more about the unique ecology of Western North Carolina and are excited to teach as an Adult Education Specialist at The NC Arboretum. As a local Asheville artist\, Meg creates zines\, collages\, and mixed-media works that explore human emotion\, personal growth\, and our search for meaning. Nature is a constant source of inspiration in their creative practice\, and they delight in experimenting with new materials\, techniques\, and art forms. \n●  Pre-registration is required for all classes unless otherwise detailed above. \n●  Members: Remember to enter your promo code upon checkout to receive your 10% member discount! Email adulteducation@ncarboretum.org with questions. \n●  See a full listing of Adult Education Programs at the North Carolina Arboretum here! Questions? Check our FAQs. \n●  We are committed to fostering a diverse\, supportive learning community. If the cost of a class is a barrier for you\, please complete this scholarship form and we will see what kind of assistance we can provide. \n●  Follow us on Facebook & Instagram! Or join our Adult Education Student Hub on Discord! \n●  Interested in supporting the NC Arboretum more directly? Donate to the ONE Fund here. \nSee all of our classes open for registration here!
URL:https://ncarboretum.org/event/soundscapes-january/
